<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0661">Remark <span data-tag="0661">9.3.4.22</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $\mathscr {F}: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{&lt; \lambda }$ be a flat functor. Then for every $n$-truncated object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the Kan complex $\mathscr {F}(C)$ is $n$-truncated. To prove this, we can apply Lemma <a data-tag="0660" href="/tag/0660">9.3.4.21</a> (possibly after enlarging $\lambda $) to realize $\mathscr {F}$ as a levelwise filtered colimit of corepresentable functors. Since the collection of $n$-truncated Kan complexes is closed under filtered colimits (Variant <a data-tag="0631" href="/tag/0631">9.1.9.3</a>), we may assume without loss of generality that $\mathscr {F}$ is corepresentable by an object $D \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. In this case, we are reduced to the assertion that the every morphism space $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( D,C )$ is $n$-truncated, which follows from our assumption on $C$. A similar argument shows that $\mathscr {F}$ carries $n$-truncated morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ to $n$-truncated morphisms of Kan complexes. See Proposition <a data-tag="065D" href="/tag/065D">9.3.2.17</a> and Remark <a data-tag="05EP" href="/tag/05EP">7.1.4.10</a> for related assertions. </p>
</article>
